From: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>
Subject: [PATCH] ath11k: fix the REO descriptor lifetime on tid delete

Two places let a freed REO queue descriptor stay reachable.

ath11k_dp_reo_cmd_list_cleanup() walks three lists. The second and third
release the descriptor with dma_free_noncoherent() over
rx_tid->unaligned_size and rx_tid->paddr_unaligned, which is how
ath11k_dp_rx_tid_buf_alloc() produced it. The first, over
reo_cmd_update_rx_queue_list, instead calls dma_unmap_single() on the
aligned address and size and then kfree()s the result of
dma_alloc_noncoherent(). Neither call matches the allocation, and the
correct sequence is written twice in the same function ten lines below.

ath11k_peer_rx_tid_delete() copies the peer's dp_rx_tid into the queue
element and then rebinds its local rx_tid pointer to elem->data inside
the walk. Everything it clears afterwards - vaddr_unaligned, paddr,
size, pending_desc_size, unaligned_size - therefore lands on the
element's copy, and the peer keeps active = true plus the address of a
descriptor the element now owns and will free. ath11k_peer_rx_tid_cleanup()
calls this and then ath11k_dp_rx_frags_cleanup() on the same peer entry,
and a second delete for the tid passes the "if (!rx_tid->active)" gate
and queues the same descriptor twice.

Clear the peer's copy where ownership transfers, right after the memcpy.
The frag fields are deliberately left alone: ath11k_dp_rx_frags_cleanup()
is the next call and still needs dst_ring_desc and rx_frag_list.

The first two hunks are API pairings with one correct answer. The third
is the least mechanical of the set - it decides that the queue element
owns the descriptor after the copy, which is what the rest of the
function already assumes - and wants a device pass.

Signed-off-by: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>

diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/dp_rx.c b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/dp_rx.c
index ab1e5a0..666903e 100644
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/dp_rx.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/dp_rx.c
@@ -746,9 +746,9 @@
 		list_del(&cmd_queue->list);
 		rx_tid = &cmd_queue->data;
 		if (rx_tid->vaddr_unaligned) {
-			dma_unmap_single(ab->dev, rx_tid->paddr,
-					 rx_tid->size, DMA_BIDIRECTIONAL);
-			kfree(rx_tid->vaddr_unaligned);
+			dma_free_noncoherent(ab->dev, rx_tid->unaligned_size,
+					     rx_tid->vaddr_unaligned,
+					     rx_tid->paddr_unaligned, DMA_BIDIRECTIONAL);
 			rx_tid->vaddr_unaligned = NULL;
 		}
 		kfree(cmd_queue);
@@ -950,6 +950,19 @@
 	elem->peer_id = peer->peer_id;
 	elem->tid = tid;
 	memcpy(&elem->data, rx_tid, sizeof(*rx_tid));
+
+	/* The queue element owns the REO descriptor from here on, and the loop
+	 * below rebinds rx_tid to elem->data - so the clears it performs never
+	 * reach the peer. Drop the peer's second reference to the same
+	 * descriptor now, leaving the frag fields for
+	 * ath11k_dp_rx_frags_cleanup(), which its caller runs next.
+	 */
+	rx_tid->active = false;
+	rx_tid->vaddr_unaligned = NULL;
+	rx_tid->paddr = 0;
+	rx_tid->size = 0;
+	rx_tid->pending_desc_size = 0;
+	rx_tid->unaligned_size = 0;
 
 	spin_lock_bh(&dp->reo_cmd_update_queue_lock);
 	list_add_tail(&elem->list, &dp->reo_cmd_update_rx_queue_list);
